+# Decision: Concurrency Policy for Independently Edited Visits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers submit visit edits from tablets that can be offline for up to 48 hours. Two workers sometimes correct the same visit independently, starting from the same server state before either submission is accepted. The record store must decide what happens when that occurs: whether the server attempts to combine the two edits into one current record, or defers every such case to a human.
+
+A visit's scientific fields — observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes — are not independent in ways the server can see from field names alone. A correction to a species identification and a correction to that species' cover estimate are substantively related even though they live in different fields; the server has no general way to distinguish that case from two edits that are genuinely unrelated, such as fixing an observer's name and adding a missing species entry. This is the crux the decision turns on.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Gate (inherited) — No submitted edit is discarded.** From the HLD tenet "Do not silently discard a submitted edit." Any option must retain every original submission regardless of how (or whether) it is resolved into a current record.
+- **Criterion (major) — Risk of silently combining scientifically related edits.** Tied to the HLD tenet "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable." An automatic combination that crosses a scientific relationship the server doesn't recognize produces an exported value that looks resolved but was never actually vetted by someone who understood the relationship — and it surfaces, if at all, only after the partner has the data.
+- **Criterion (major) — Review workload imposed on the two coordinators.** Tied to the HLD tenet "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator." The team runs on two people with no dedicated review staff; what reaches their queue every week is a direct cost, not a background concern.
+- **Criterion (moderate) — Implementation and ongoing maintenance burden.** Merge logic for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries has to be written once and kept correct as field types are added later.
+- **Criterion (minor) — Submission size over degraded connectivity.** Tied to the HLD tenet "Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity." At this project's scale (visits are small structured records, not media), submission size is a minor consideration rather than a binding constraint.
+
+Latent criteria considered and set aside: submission latency and server-side compute cost don't differentiate the options at this record volume (roughly 6,000 visits/season); neither does auditability of the raw submissions, since both options retain every original submission under the preservation gate.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each submission names the base revision it started editing from, alongside the worker's complete visit payload. A submission whose base revision matches the visit's current accepted revision becomes the new current revision. A submission whose base revision is contested by another submission on the same base becomes a competing revision instead: neither becomes current, and the visit carries unresolved review work until a coordinator resolves it by designating (or composing) the accepted outcome.
+
+Demonstration: two workers edit visit V from revision r1. Worker A corrects the observer name; worker B adds a missing species entry. Despite touching unrelated fields, both submissions name base r1, so both become competing revisions and the visit enters review. A coordinator reconciles manually.
+
+Analysis:
+- Preservation gate: **passes** — both competing revisions are retained regardless of outcome.
+- Risk of silent incorrect combination: **strong fit** — no automatic combination ever occurs, so there is no field-relationship judgment the server could get wrong.
+- Review workload: **weak fit** — every contested pair reaches review, including pairs that touch obviously unrelated fields, inflating the queue the two coordinators work through each week.
+- Implementation/maintenance burden: **strong fit** — no merge rules to write or keep correct as field types evolve.
+- Submission size: **partial fit** — every submission carries the full visit payload rather than a change set, though payload size is not binding at this scale.
+
+Summary: trades a larger, undifferentiated review queue for zero automatic-combination risk and minimal merge logic.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+Each submission supplies its base state and a structured change set rather than a full payload. Disjoint field changes between competing submissions combine automatically into one current revision; changes that overlap on the same field go to coordinator review.
+
+Demonstration: the same scenario — worker A changes only the observer name, worker B changes only the species list. The fields are disjoint by name, so field-aware merge combines both changes into one current revision automatically, with no coordinator ever seeing it. A genuinely related pair — a species identification correction and a cover-estimate correction for that species — would also be treated as disjoint by field name and combined automatically, despite being substantively linked.
+
+Analysis:
+- Preservation gate: **passes** — original submissions are retained; only the current/accepted representation differs from either individual submission.
+- Risk of silent incorrect combination: **weak fit** — this is exactly the failure mode the option cannot distinguish: two edits that are disjoint by field name but related in scientific substance combine without any human seeing it.
+- Review workload: **strong fit** — only genuinely overlapping edits reach the coordinators; disjoint edits resolve without review.
+- Implementation/maintenance burden: **weak fit** — requires maintained merge rules per field shape (scalar fields, observer lists, species entries), with new rules needed as field types are added.
+- Submission size: **strong fit** — change-only submissions are smaller than full payloads.
+
+Summary: trades implementation burden and the silent-combination risk for a smaller review queue and smaller submissions.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions**, chosen.
+
+The deciding criterion is the risk of silently combining scientifically related edits. Field-aware merge cannot distinguish "disjoint by field name" from "disjoint in substance" without a scientific model the project has explicitly ruled out building (the HLD non-goal excludes "automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent") — so any merge-rule set sophisticated enough to catch species-identification/cover-estimate style relationships would itself be exactly the kind of inference the project does not want the server attempting. Whole-visit revisions sidestep the question entirely by never combining anything without a coordinator present, which is what "keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable" requires here: every value the partner receives traces to a submission a human looked at, not an inferred combination.
+
+Review workload is also a major criterion, and it points the other way — whole-visit revisions send every contested pair to review, including ones that touch obviously unrelated fields. The team accepted this cost explicitly: a bigger review queue is tolerable workload for two coordinators to absorb, whereas an undetected bad automatic merge is not something they can review their way out of, since by construction nothing flags it for review. Implementation and maintenance burden reinforces the same direction — no merge rules to write or keep correct as field types evolve. Submission size favors field-aware merge but is minor and does not move the decision.
+
+**What this forecloses:** no visit will ever reach an exported state through an automatic combination of two submissions; every multi-submission visit becomes current only through a single accepted revision (either the one whose base revision was current, or a coordinator's resolution).
+
+**What this obligates downstream:**
+- Tablet-sync must retain base revision IDs locally so each submission can name the revision it started from, and must be able to display to a worker that their submission is a pending competing revision rather than silently accepted.
+- Review-export must provide a revision-comparison workflow for coordinators (showing competing revisions side by side so a resolution can be composed) and must exclude visits with unresolved competing revisions from the weekly export set.
+
+**When to revisit:** this decision is worth re-litigating if either side of its central trade-off shifts materially — if the review queue whole-visit revisions produce grows beyond what two coordinators can sustain as the survey scales, or if a narrow, human-vetted set of field-level merge rules could be defined for genuinely independent fields without approaching scientific inference (for example, rules scoped and signed off with the science partner rather than inferred by the server). Neither condition holds today.
